It's like an L-shape of the floor plan of the 12th floor, but we go in, and he's down at one end, and we go into Rob's office, and I remember Rob, this is how young we were, we had our resumes attached to the thing, and they were like, and Rob, I remember Rob being like, well, their grades are really good.
这就像 12 楼平面图的 L 形，但我们进去了，他在一端，我们进入罗布的办公室，我记得罗布，我们当时多么年轻，我们把简历贴在这个东西上，他们就像，罗布，我记得罗布说，嗯，他们的成绩真的很好。

00:26:17.809 --> 00:26:19.809
We got a GPA.
我们有平均绩点（GPA）。

00:26:19.809 --> 00:26:22.450
They're like, good GPAs.
他们的平均绩点（GPA）很好。

00:26:22.450 --> 00:26:25.609
But yeah, in that moment, he was like, this is on the month of October.
但是，是的，在那一刻，他就像，这是十月。

00:26:25.609 --> 00:26:26.609
Yeah.
是的。

00:26:26.609 --> 00:26:27.609
Not bad.
不错。

00:26:27.609 --> 00:26:28.609
I know CPR.
我知道心肺复苏术。

00:26:28.609 --> 00:26:30.410
I don't know CPR.
我不懂心肺复苏术。

00:26:30.410 --> 00:26:36.490
But, but that was on a Friday and he basically said, like, they seemed good to me.
但是，那是在星期五，他基本上说，他们对我来说似乎很好。

00:26:36.490 --> 00:26:39.049
Then he turned to us and it was like, enjoy this weekend.
然后他转向我们，就像，享受这个周末。

00:26:39.049 --> 00:26:42.849
This is as good as this job is going to get because starting Monday is going to be terrible.
这已经是这份工作所能得到的最好的结果了，因为从周一开始将会很糟糕。

00:26:42.849 --> 00:26:45.450
But, you know, come in on Monday and you're hired.
但是，你知道，周一来你就会被录用。

00:26:45.450 --> 00:26:57.829
And we walked out and we're so stunned that we got lost in the complex L shape floor plan of the toes and no way to get lost, but we got lost all the way down the hall.
我们走出去，惊呆了，我们在脚趾复杂的L形平面图中迷路了，没有办法迷路，但我们沿着走廊一路迷路。

00:26:57.829 --> 00:27:01.930
We turned the only corner, we turned the corner, the only corner of the whole hallway.
我们拐过唯一的拐角，我们拐过拐角，整个走廊唯一的拐角。

00:27:01.930 --> 00:27:06.609
And we turn the corner and like you can film this in your mind, it's it's high noon.
我们转过拐角，就像你可以在脑海中拍摄这一幕一样，现在是正午。

00:27:06.609 --> 00:27:13.849
And there are the other end is David Letterman, like standing alone, like I think he was like tossing a football, just standing there.
另一端是大卫·莱特曼，就像独自站着一样，我认为他就像扔一个足球一样，只是站在那里。

00:27:13.849 --> 00:27:20.359
like he's maybe going to kick our ass or something, and we say to him, do you know where the elevator is?
就像他可能会踢我们的屁股什么的，我们对他说，你知道电梯在哪里吗？

00:27:20.359 --> 00:27:22.960
And he says, it doesn't fall.
他说，它不会掉落。

00:27:22.960 --> 00:27:27.839
And like I love, I remember that particular phrasing, do you know where the elevator is?
就像我喜欢的那样，我记得那个特定的措辞，你知道电梯在哪里吗？

00:27:27.839 --> 00:27:30.960
Like this building that has your name on it in giant lights?
喜欢这座用巨型灯光刻有您名字的建筑吗？

00:27:30.960 --> 00:27:31.960
Wow.
哇。

00:27:31.960 --> 00:27:32.960
You do it the other way.
你用另一种方式来做。

00:27:32.960 --> 00:27:34.839
That's an incredibly charming story.
这是一个极其迷人的故事。

00:27:34.839 --> 00:27:38.880
I love that you guys read in him and this mythic kind of character.
我喜欢你们读到他和这个神话般的人物。

00:27:38.880 --> 00:27:39.880
Yeah.
是的。

00:27:39.880 --> 00:27:40.880
And I think we were wearing backpacks.
我想我们都背着背包。

00:27:40.880 --> 00:27:42.079
I think we brought backpacks in the meaning.
我想我们带背包就是这个意思。

00:27:42.079 --> 00:27:44.839
He called us the backpack boys behind our back for years.
多年来他在背后称我们为背包男孩。

00:27:44.839 --> 00:27:55.599
Yeah, the backpack was our nickname, the worst boy, the worst boy man of all time, the, the rough draft is the most be boys and the most be boys, so you guys are there.
是的，背包是我们的昵称，最糟糕的男孩，有史以来最糟糕的男孩，粗略的选秀是最男孩和最男孩，所以你们都在那里。

00:27:55.599 --> 00:27:56.599
How long?
多长时间？

00:27:56.599 --> 00:27:57.599
Six years.
六年了。

00:27:57.599 --> 00:27:58.599
Four and a half years.
四年半了。

00:27:58.599 --> 00:27:59.599
Oh, four and a half.
哦，四岁半。

00:27:59.599 --> 00:28:00.599
Four and a half.
四点半。

00:28:00.599 --> 00:28:01.599
Okay.
好的。

00:28:01.599 --> 00:28:07.440
The next time we talk today, after that story was just when we said goodbye, we probably talked about three other times in between.
今天我们下次聊的时候，在那个故事之后，就在我们告别的时候，我们中间可能还聊了另外三个话题。

00:28:07.440 --> 00:28:15.680
Tell me, what did you guys learn at Letterman that helped you write and run how I met your mother?
告诉我，你们在莱特曼学到了什么来帮助你们编写和运行《我如何遇见你们母亲》？

00:28:15.680 --> 00:28:16.920
Everything, right, Carter?
一切，对吧，卡特？

00:28:16.920 --> 00:28:18.920
Like, everything.
就像，一切。

00:28:18.920 --> 00:28:26.240
After Letterman, we were on a couple of other sitcoms that are sort of forgettable, didn't really go the distance.
在《莱特曼》之后，我们还出演了其他几部情景喜剧，这些情景喜剧有点令人忘记，并没有真正走下去。

00:28:26.240 --> 00:28:28.960
Although we're an American dad, which is still on TV, which is amazing.
虽然我们是美国爸爸，但这仍然在电视上，这太棒了。

00:28:28.960 --> 00:28:30.359
We're in there for one season.
我们在那里呆了一个赛季。

00:28:30.359 --> 00:28:34.720
But basically, everything we needed to learn to be a showrunner, we learned at Letterman.
但基本上，我们在莱特曼学到了成为一名制片人所需的一切。

00:28:34.720 --> 00:28:43.059
And then when you go on to a sitcom staff, a lot of the ethos at least back in the day when we were getting into it was like the writers weren't in the edit room.
然后当你继续接触情景喜剧的工作人员时，至少在我们进入它的那天，很多精神就像编剧不在编辑室一样。

00:28:43.059 --> 00:28:44.740
The writers weren't necessarily always on set.
编剧不一定总是在片场。

00:28:44.740 --> 00:28:48.900
The writers sort of wrote things and were that part of the assembly line.
作家们写了一些东西，并且是流水线的一部分。

00:28:48.900 --> 00:28:54.160
On Letterman, you had, if you had an idea in the morning, you had to produce it by the showtime.
在莱特曼身上，如果你早上有一个想法，你就必须在放映前将其付诸实践。

00:28:54.160 --> 00:28:55.160
You had to get your music.
你必须得到你的音乐。

00:28:55.160 --> 00:28:56.160
You had to order up your graphics.
你必须订购你的图形。

00:28:56.160 --> 00:28:59.400
You had to, if there was like Kiron and like titles involved, you had every piece of it.
你必须这么做，如果有像《Kiron》之类的游戏，你就拥有了它的每一个部分。

00:28:59.400 --> 00:29:00.400
You went on the shoot.
你去拍摄了。

00:29:00.400 --> 00:29:02.720
You basically kind of directed or co-directed your bit.
你基本上是导演或共同导演你的部分。

00:29:02.720 --> 00:29:14.359
you had with the director and make sure you got everything you needed, get into an edit room, book your time there, get with the editor, like every part of it was you, every part of it you were responsible for producing.
你和导演在一起，确保你得到了你需要的一切，进入剪辑室，在那里预订时间，和剪辑师在一起，就像它的每个部分都是你，你负责制作它的每个部分一样。

00:29:14.359 --> 00:29:28.200
And that was such good training for being a showrunner because by the time, I think by the time some writers who've only ever been on sitcom staffs, they become a showrunner, they've never really spent a lot of time in edit, they've never done a sound mix, they've never, we had done all that stuff.
对于成为一名剧集主管来说，这是一次很好的培训，因为到了那个时候，我想当一些只在情景喜剧工作人员中工作过的作家成为一名剧集主管时，他们从来没有真正花很多时间在编辑上，他们从来没有做过混音，他们从来没有，我们做过所有这些事情。

00:29:28.200 --> 00:29:35.920
It can't be overstated how much like technology and like the editing software that exists in a like eye movie coming built into your laptop.
无论如何强调，它与笔记本电脑内置的类似眼睛电影中的技术和编辑软件有多么相似。

00:29:35.920 --> 00:29:44.480
How much that has changed the world because at the time it really was like us knowing how to go into an edit room was like like we'd gone to Hogwarts.
这对世界产生了多大的改变，因为当时我们真的知道如何进入编辑室就像我们去了霍格沃茨一样。

00:29:44.480 --> 00:29:56.920
Like we like had this like mystical power that like like really like no one understood how to like you know trim 15 frames off of something like like dumb simple stuff that anyone in the world now knows.
就像我们喜欢拥有这种神秘的力量，就像真的没有人知道如何像你知道的那样，从世界上任何人现在都知道的愚蠢简单的东西中剪掉 15 帧。

00:29:56.920 --> 00:30:04.240
It was really that letterman definitely gave us a chance to basically go to film school for four years.
确实莱特曼确实给了我们一个基本上去电影学校读四年的机会。

00:30:04.240 --> 00:30:07.359
It was film school and comedy school in a big way.
从很大程度上来说，这是电影学校和喜剧学校。

00:30:07.359 --> 00:30:21.640
I also feel like the whole 10,000 hours thing of learning how to, I definitely feel like I learned how to write a joke and how to write something that gets a laugh.
我也觉得在学习如何写一万个小时的过程中，我确实觉得我学会了如何写一个笑话以及如何写一些让人发笑的东西。

00:30:21.640 --> 00:30:31.119
I don't even think that myself is necessarily a funny person, but I knew what the mechanics were of had, and the only way to learn that is doing it over and over and failing a lot.
我什至不认为自己一定是一个有趣的人，但我知道其中的机制是什么，而学习这一点的唯一方法就是一遍又一遍地做，并多次失败。

00:30:31.119 --> 00:30:34.300
And like, yeah, I wanted to ask you about the failure.
就像，是的，我想问你关于失败的事情。

00:30:34.300 --> 00:30:37.245
Did it, it did it, thicken your skin considerably.
做到了，做到了，让你的脸皮显着变厚。

00:30:37.245 --> 00:30:47.966
So you, you pitch jokes, obviously, to these older, more veteran writers, and they're swatting them down or ideas are getting, you know, batted down or something doesn't get a laugh.
所以，你，显然，你向这些年长、更有经验的作家讲笑话，他们正在猛烈地抨击这些笑话，或者你知道，你的想法受到了打击，或者有些东西没有得到笑声。

00:30:47.966 --> 00:30:53.680
Like, did you feel like that, which didn't kill you made you stronger kind of by the end of that?
就像，你有没有这样的感觉，这并没有杀死你，但到最后却让你变得更坚强？

00:30:53.680 --> 00:30:54.548
Yeah, definitely.
是的，绝对是。

00:30:54.548 --> 00:30:57.920
And it was very humbling and it told you it was a volume business.
这是非常令人谦卑的，它告诉你这是一项批量业务。

00:30:57.920 --> 00:30:59.342
You needed to have an extra joke.
你需要一个额外的笑话。

00:30:59.342 --> 00:31:00.764
You needed to have an extra idea.
你需要有一个额外的想法。

00:31:00.764 --> 00:31:05.980
There was no, there's no writers block when you're doing five shows a week and you're getting paid decent money to do it.
不，当你每周做五场演出并且你得到了可观的报酬时，就没有作家的障碍。

00:31:05.980 --> 00:31:06.756
There's no writer.
没有作家。

00:31:06.756 --> 00:31:07.833
You have to have an idea.
你必须有一个想法。

00:31:07.833 --> 00:31:08.911
You have to have an idea.
你必须有一个想法。

00:31:08.911 --> 00:31:13.997
And even if it's not great, you have to do, you have to produce it and make it and hope that it gets on and does okay.
即使它不是很好，你也必须这样做，你必须生产它并制作它，并希望它能够顺利进行并表现良好。

00:31:13.997 --> 00:31:17.920
Carter and I did the viewer mail segment for four years of our four and a half years there.
卡特和我在那里工作了四年半，其中四年半都在做观众邮件部分。

00:31:17.920 --> 00:31:21.651
And you, we would go down for the taping of that segment and be in the audience.
而你，我们会去录制该片段并成为观众。

00:31:21.651 --> 00:31:33.920
And you had to sit in that audience and we stood up to the side and we would sit there and listen to those played and either get a laugh from the audience or not get a laugh from the audience and what didn't get a laugh and it was dead silent and Dave's up there.
你必须坐在观众席上，我们站在一边，我们会坐在那里听那些演奏的音乐，要么让观众笑，要么不让观众笑，如果没有笑，那就是一片死寂，而戴夫就在那里。

00:31:33.920 --> 00:31:39.160
This iconic, wonderful, I mean, I idolized Dave, I love him, I made that joke before, but I love Dave.
这个标志性的，美妙的，我的意思是，我崇拜戴夫，我爱他，我以前开过那个玩笑，但我爱戴夫。

00:31:39.160 --> 00:31:42.440
When he would turn on it, he'd go like, oh, that was a tough one, Paul.
当他打开它时，他会说，哦，这很难，保罗。

00:31:42.440 --> 00:31:49.279
I feel like we're getting torn a new one on national television by our hero or at least my hero.
我觉得我们的英雄，或者至少是我的英雄，正在国家电视台上撕扯一场新的戏。

00:31:49.279 --> 00:31:51.013
And there's nothing more humbling.
没有什么比这更令人谦卑的了。

00:31:51.013 --> 00:31:53.920
There's no thicker skin to be developed than that moment.
没有比那一刻更厚的脸皮了。

00:31:53.920 --> 00:31:58.720
And you got to scrape yourself off the floor and say, I got at least one more shot at this next week if we don't get fired.
你必须把自己从地板上爬起来，说，如果我们不被解雇的话，下周我至少还有一次机会。

00:31:59.200 --> 00:32:00.983
And you just work even harder.
而你只是更加努力地工作。

00:32:00.983 --> 00:32:03.599
And you develop that ability to bounce back.
你就会培养出反弹的能力。

